1. An electronic cigarette control unit comprising:  

   a power source comprising a rechargeable battery;  

   an electrical connector configured to electrically couple the control unit to a heater for heating an aerosol-forming substrate;  

   a controller operably coupled to the rechargeable battery and to the electrical connector, the controller being configured to control delivery of electrical power from the rechargeable battery to the heater during use of the electronic cigarette;  

   a memory storing usage data associated with operation of the electronic cigarette, the usage data comprising at least one of puff count, puff duration, heater activation time, power delivered to the heater, battery state of charge, battery voltage, battery current, temperature, elapsed time, or historical recharging information;  

   wherein the controller is configured to process the usage data to predict whether the rechargeable battery will reach a low-battery condition before a subsequent recharging opportunity or before completion of a predicted future usage interval; and  

   wherein, in response to predicting that the rechargeable battery will reach the low-battery condition, the controller is configured to cause at least one low-battery response comprising providing a notification to a user or modifying operation of the electronic cigarette to reduce power consumption.

1. **An electronic cigarette battery management system**, comprising:  
   a rechargeable battery configured to supply electrical power to a heating element of an electronic cigarette;  
   a sensor circuit configured to collect operation data associated with use of the electronic cigarette, the operation data including at least one of puff frequency, puff duration, heating element activation time, battery voltage, battery current, battery temperature, or remaining battery capacity;  
   a recharge detection circuit configured to collect recharge data associated with recharging of the rechargeable battery, the recharge data including at least one of recharge start time, recharge end time, recharge duration, recharge current, recharge voltage, state of charge before recharging, or state of charge after recharging;  
   a memory configured to store the operation data and the recharge data;  
   a processor operatively coupled to the memory and configured to:  
   update a battery usage model based at least in part on the collected operation data and the collected recharge data;  
   determine, using the updated battery usage model, a predicted battery discharge profile for a period following a current time;  
   predict whether the rechargeable battery will discharge below a predetermined battery threshold before a next expected recharge event; and  
   generate a user notification in response to predicting that the rechargeable battery will discharge below the predetermined battery threshold before the next expected recharge event; and  
   a user interface configured to output the user notification.

2. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the battery usage model includes a user-specific usage pattern model generated from historical puff behavior and historical recharge behavior.

3. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the processor is further configured to estimate the next expected recharge event based on at least one prior recharge time, recharge interval, time of day, day of week, or location-associated charging pattern.

4. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the predetermined battery threshold comprises a minimum state-of-charge value required to support a predicted number of future puff events before the next expected recharge event.

5. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the user notification comprises at least one of a visual indication, an audible indication, a haptic indication, a message displayed on a connected mobile device, or a wireless notification transmitted to an external device.

6. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the processor is further configured to update the battery usage model after each puff event and after each recharge event.

7. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the predicted battery discharge profile is determined based on a measured battery capacity degradation characteristic of the rechargeable battery.

8. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the operation data further includes power delivered to the heating element during each activation of the electronic cigarette.

9. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the recharge data further includes a charge acceptance characteristic indicative of battery aging.

10. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the processor is further configured to modify the predetermined battery threshold based on observed variability in user consumption.

11. **The system of claim 1**, wherein the user notification includes a recommendation to recharge the electronic cigarette before the predicted discharge below the predetermined battery threshold.

12. **A method of managing a battery of an electronic cigarette**, comprising:  
   collecting operation data during use of the electronic cigarette;  
   collecting recharge data during recharging of a rechargeable battery of the electronic cigarette;  
   storing the operation data and the recharge data in a memory;  
   updating, by a processor, a battery usage model based on the stored operation data and recharge data;  
   predicting, using the updated battery usage model, whether the rechargeable battery will discharge below a predetermined threshold before a next expected recharge event; and  
   notifying a user when the rechargeable battery is predicted to discharge below the predetermined threshold before the next expected recharge event.

13. **The method of claim 12**, further comprising estimating the next expected recharge event from historical recharge data.

14. **The method of claim 12**, further comprising determining a predicted number of puff events occurring before the next expected recharge event.

15. **The method of claim 12**, wherein updating the battery usage model includes adjusting a predicted energy consumption per puff based on measured battery voltage drop during prior puff events.

16. **The method of claim 12**, wherein notifying the user includes transmitting a wireless alert to a mobile computing device paired with the electronic cigarette.

17. **A non-transitory computer-readable medium** storing instructions that, when executed by a processor of an electronic cigarette or a device communicatively coupled thereto, cause the processor to:  
   receive operation data relating to activation of a heating element of the electronic cigarette;  
   receive recharge data relating to charging of a rechargeable battery of the electronic cigarette;  
   update a battery usage prediction model using the operation data and recharge data;  
   determine a predicted time at which the rechargeable battery will fall below a predetermined charge threshold;  
   determine a next expected recharge time; and  
   cause a user notification to be output when the predicted time occurs before the next expected recharge time.
